00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:32.120
And finally, customer service.

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:35.550
By helping through
different use cases,

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:39.440
both on the front line
and backup house support,

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:41.540
patients feel that
they're cared for

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:45.360
and they're able to get their
questions answered quickly.

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:48.350
So now that we talked about
how the solution works,

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:51.435
let's talk about what some of
the actual use cases for Chrome

00:15:51.435 --> 00:15:53.810
and health care looks like
and what some of our customers

00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:57.020
are doing today, starting with
the mobile health care worker.

00:15:57.020 --> 00:15:58.700
In this use case,
we're typically

00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:01.700
deploying Chrome in
a one to one capacity

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:06.290
on a tablet, a Chromebook, or a
two in one convertible device.

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:11.300
And this really looks like the
clinical plus knowledge worker

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:16.910
hybrid solution or
mobile VDI, if you will.

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:20.960
And again, all of
the major OEMs,

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:23.880
you can find one of those
form factors from them.

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:27.500
One thing that we used
to hear is that's great

00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:29.760
and I'm really
liking the solution,

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:33.560
but I need for the provider
to be able to work even when

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:35.330
they're not on a Wi-Fi network.

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:38.330
And the good news is that
we do have LTE options

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:40.760
in the hardware portfolio
today so that you can have

00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:44.660
that connectivity at any time.

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:46.110
Shared workstations.

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:48.410
So this looks like
clinical workflow.

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:50.250
Multiple people are
sharing one device.

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:54.560
It might be in a hallway, in an
exam room, on a medical cart.

00:16:54.560 --> 00:17:01.550
That could be in an all in one,
on a Chromebox or a Chromebook.

00:17:01.550 --> 00:17:04.790
And the wonderful news
here is that we're

00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:07.910
able to complete clinical
workflow and the shared user

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:10.010
workspace through
our partnerships

00:17:10.010 --> 00:17:12.950
with Imprivata and HealthCast.

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:15.500
So where that badge tap
and go is concerned,

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:20.060
we actually do have a demo of
it at the health care industry

00:17:20.060 --> 00:17:22.713
breakout on that side
of the showcase floor.

00:17:22.713 --> 00:17:24.380
So definitely encourage
you to check out

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:26.630
what our shared
workstation use case looks

00:17:26.630 --> 00:17:28.960
like after the session today.

00:17:28.960 --> 00:17:31.730
Digitize training
and communications.

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:35.720
Fun fact, training
is a common use case

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:38.990
where a lot of customers start
their journey with Chrome.

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:44.510
The reason for that is because,
typically, training departments

00:17:44.510 --> 00:17:47.480
have a smaller pool
of devices that

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:49.350
are in a siloed environment.

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:53.420
And so that silo creates
a more palatable situation

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:57.500
to run a POC and pilot.

00:17:57.500 --> 00:18:01.340
And a fun fact for you, as well,
or an anecdote, I should say.

00:18:01.340 --> 00:18:04.010
One of our customers
from Wisconsin

00:18:04.010 --> 00:18:08.780
actually worked with us at
HIMSS19 in Orlando in February

00:18:08.780 --> 00:18:11.690
and shared that in
their training use case,

00:18:11.690 --> 00:18:14.330
they were preparing
for incoming residents

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:17.870
for the next educational season.

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:23.510
And they, by their own measure,
saved 150 hours in time

00:18:23.510 --> 00:18:27.890
from not having to image
devices but rather enroll them

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:30.680
into the management console.

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:33.680
So they felt like those
were really great results.

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:36.200
And as such, they
have now started

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:38.120
to look at Chrome
for other use cases.

00:18:40.790 --> 00:18:43.150
And finally, temporary staff.

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:48.340
Has anyone here ever forgotten
their laptop at work?

00:18:48.340 --> 00:18:49.480
Couple of people.

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:51.140
I actually did it two weeks ago.

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:52.490
I was really embarrassed.

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:55.120
But the great news
is that Google has

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:57.040
a solution called grab and go.

00:18:57.040 --> 00:19:01.490
And our IT department
created it a while ago.

00:19:01.490 --> 00:19:04.420
And the reason why they did
it was to help with end user

00:19:04.420 --> 00:19:06.040
productivity uptime.

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:07.990
So in our grab and
go solution, which

00:19:07.990 --> 00:19:10.510
we are demoing at the
Chrome Enterprise breakout

00:19:10.510 --> 00:19:13.180
in the workplace transformation
space, what happens

00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:16.630
is that an end user will go
to a hardware kiosk, which

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:20.680
is available through one of
our hardware-- or our partners.

00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:24.460
And they check out the device,
log in through their cloud base

00:19:24.460 --> 00:19:26.650
profile, and they're
up and running

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:28.180
for the day or the
week, if they're

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:29.900
visiting another location.

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:32.230
And then once they're
done, they simply

00:19:32.230 --> 00:19:36.010
log out and return the
device to the hardware kiosk.

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:37.840
Now, you might be
thinking, well,

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:39.110
what does that actually mean?

00:19:39.110 --> 00:19:42.520
Notice in this situation
that I just described,

00:19:42.520 --> 00:19:46.540
there was no provisioning
required by IT for that end

00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:49.060
user to start doing their
job, which meant that they

00:19:49.060 --> 00:19:51.340
were able to start immediately.

00:19:51.340 --> 00:19:53.440
And there was no
de-provisioning time

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:56.230
needed for the IT
department, which

00:19:56.230 --> 00:19:59.740
means that the end user
experienced more uptime

00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:02.140
or access to their information
and the IT department

00:20:02.140 --> 00:20:06.040
won because they did not have to
serve that particular incident.

00:20:06.040 --> 00:20:08.170
So grab and go is a
wonderful solution.

00:20:08.170 --> 00:20:12.070
And maybe you have health
care vendors that you serve

00:20:12.070 --> 00:20:13.840
or other people that
rotate in and out

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:15.080
throughout your facility.

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:18.630
So definitely check this out
at the workplace transformation

00:20:18.630 --> 00:20:21.160
booth.

00:20:21.160 --> 00:20:23.740
Moving over to the
patient facing use case.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:25.960
We have two examples
to talk through today.

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:28.590
The first being self-service.

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:30.460
In the self-service
use case, this

00:20:30.460 --> 00:20:34.330
is where we're talking about
kiosks being used for patient

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:38.290
check in and check
out, to take surveys,

00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:41.470
to verify address
information, insurance.

00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:44.380
When the patient is able
to do this on their own,

00:20:44.380 --> 00:20:49.450
that saves on administrative
time from back office staff,

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:51.460
freeing them up to
do other things.

00:20:51.460 --> 00:20:52.960
And it also is nice
for the patient,

00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:54.910
because they're in
more direct control

00:20:54.910 --> 00:20:57.590
of their own information.

00:20:57.590 --> 00:21:01.870
And then finally, the in room
entertainment and information

00:21:01.870 --> 00:21:03.040
use case.

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:08.450
This might look like a
tablet at the bedside.

00:21:08.450 --> 00:21:13.210
It might look like a Chromebook
being used in a waiting area.

00:21:13.210 --> 00:21:18.040
Or it might look like a
Chromebox attached to a monitor

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:19.690
in a hospital room.

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:24.370
And in this particular use
case, a patient facing app

00:21:24.370 --> 00:21:25.990
would be delivered
to the patient

00:21:25.990 --> 00:21:29.020
so that they have
more direct access

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:30.550
to their own information.

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:37.780
Lab results, the list of their
clinic visits, different things

00:21:37.780 --> 00:21:41.590
that they would want to have
access to about their care plan

00:21:41.590 --> 00:21:43.750
or about their treatment
or having access

00:21:43.750 --> 00:21:46.480
to directly communicate
with their care provider.

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:50.050
So there are
multiple applications

00:21:50.050 --> 00:21:52.250
available for this today.

00:21:52.250 --> 00:21:55.150
But where it comes
in intersection

00:21:55.150 --> 00:21:59.200
with Chrome is that
at the end of 2017,

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:03.640
we started to deploy the Google
Play Store across all Chrome OS

00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:04.720
devices.

00:22:04.720 --> 00:22:07.830
And just like we
talked about how

00:22:07.830 --> 00:22:11.740
we leveraged these entertainment
apps for our own use on the way

00:22:11.740 --> 00:22:15.280
to San Francisco
this week, patients

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:18.440
are still people at
the end of the day.

00:22:18.440 --> 00:22:21.040
And they want to be
able to be people

00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:22.850
and to access
different information.

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:25.510
So through the Google
Play Store on Chrome OS,

00:22:25.510 --> 00:22:29.050
you're able to deliver
some of those applications

00:22:29.050 --> 00:22:34.210
like Netflix, like Spotify
for entertainment use

00:22:34.210 --> 00:22:37.840
and help through some of
those feelings of anxiety

00:22:37.840 --> 00:22:41.080
that they might be feeling.

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:43.810
So with that said, you
just heard a lot from me

00:22:43.810 --> 00:22:45.460
about how Chrome
Enterprise works.

00:22:45.460 --> 00:22:47.680
I'm going to turn it
over to Jason Tahaney

00:22:47.680 --> 00:22:50.110
from Hunterdon Health, who
is a current Chrome customer.

00:22:50.110 --> 00:22:52.440
So he can tell you about
their Chrome journey.

00:22:52.440 --> 00:22:53.020
Jason?

00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:54.395
JASON TAHANEY:
Thank you, Angela.

00:22:54.395 --> 00:22:57.050
Hey, good afternoon, everyone.

00:22:57.050 --> 00:22:59.790
So I'm going
apologize in advance.

00:22:59.790 --> 00:23:02.350
I'm from New Jersey, and we
don't have the hills that we

00:23:02.350 --> 00:23:03.530
have here in San Francisco.

00:23:03.530 --> 00:23:05.290
We have mere foothills
compared to this,

00:23:05.290 --> 00:23:07.270
and I also took up running
about two years ago

00:23:07.270 --> 00:23:09.660
and I thought I was all that
kind of running last night

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:11.660
and my knees are not quite
what they used to be.

00:23:11.660 --> 00:23:14.683
So if I go crashing down, please
it's for no other health reason

00:23:14.683 --> 00:23:16.100
other than I was
stupid yesterday.

00:23:16.100 --> 00:23:18.820
So anyway, so I'm Jason Tahaney.

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:21.795
I'm the Director of IT
for Hunterdon Health.

00:23:21.795 --> 00:23:23.920
And a little bit about
Hunterdon Healthcare system.

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:26.470
We are a community hospital
with a very far reach.

00:23:26.470 --> 00:23:29.360
We have about 75
physician practices.

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:31.420
We are founded in 1946.

00:23:31.420 --> 00:23:33.520
Literally the miracle
in the cornfield.

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:36.190
We raised a whole bunch
of money a long time ago.

00:23:36.190 --> 00:23:38.207
And part of the Hunterdon
Healthcare system.

00:23:38.207 --> 00:23:40.540
And I'm going to talk a little
bit, as Angela mentioned,

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:42.520
about some of the Google
products that we use

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:46.910
and we've found to really be
powerful for our organization.

00:23:46.910 --> 00:23:49.750
So in health care, we
really have some serious IT

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:51.476
challenges.

00:23:51.476 --> 00:23:52.918
We have legacy applications.

00:23:52.918 --> 00:23:54.460
So unless you're
one of the lucky few

00:23:54.460 --> 00:23:57.340
and you can afford some of
the really large EMR systems,

00:23:57.340 --> 00:23:59.530
you're left with about
eight or nine different EMRs

00:23:59.530 --> 00:24:01.370
in your health system.

00:24:01.370 --> 00:24:03.550
A lot of them are
code based that

00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:05.420
was developed back in the '90s.

00:24:05.420 --> 00:24:06.940
I mean, I know that's for us.

00:24:06.940 --> 00:24:08.610
We have some of the--
some of our EMRs

00:24:08.610 --> 00:24:10.050
are just really that ancient.

00:24:10.050 --> 00:24:12.550
And not to mention even if you
have some of the larger EMRs,

00:24:12.550 --> 00:24:14.620
you still need surgical
systems and you

00:24:14.620 --> 00:24:16.990
need things like nutritional
management systems.

00:24:16.990 --> 00:24:18.730
I mean, really complex
to run a hospital.

00:24:18.730 --> 00:24:21.160
So lots of legacy applications.

00:24:21.160 --> 00:24:22.990
And then because of
legacy applications,

00:24:22.990 --> 00:24:24.620
partially, we have data silos.

00:24:24.620 --> 00:24:26.200
So we end up with
information kind

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:29.440
of all over the place across
different-- in different EMRs.

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:31.240
We try to tie it up
together with something

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:34.030
called the interface engine.

00:24:34.030 --> 00:24:37.420
And it works, but not really
quite that seamless integration

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:38.795
that we all would like to see.

00:24:38.795 --> 00:24:40.670
I mean, some of our
newer standards with FHIR

00:24:40.670 --> 00:24:42.753
that are coming out are
going to kind of hopefully

00:24:42.753 --> 00:24:44.500
help with some of those issues.

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:45.750
And then we have rising costs.

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:49.630
So I think I read, as of
2012, it's about $377 billion

00:24:49.630 --> 00:24:53.180
in costs of inpatient health
care in the United States.

00:24:53.180 --> 00:24:54.460
That's just inpatient.

00:24:54.460 --> 00:24:56.830
That's a whole lot of money.

00:24:56.830 --> 00:24:57.837
Regulatory environments.

00:24:57.837 --> 00:24:59.920
So we ask everyone here,
if you're in health care,

00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:01.300
you know what HIPAA is.

00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:02.810
Security and privacy rules.

00:25:02.810 --> 00:25:06.232
Security rules, how we protect
the privacy of our patients.

00:25:06.232 --> 00:25:07.690
We also have things
like PCI if you

00:25:07.690 --> 00:25:09.610
take financial transactions.

00:25:09.610 --> 00:25:11.752
A whole lot of PCI compliance
that you need to do.

00:25:11.752 --> 00:25:13.960
Basically if you're doing
business with credit cards,

00:25:13.960 --> 00:25:16.060
you need to be
compliant with PCI.

00:25:16.060 --> 00:25:19.090
And we have limited
skills where to start.

00:25:19.090 --> 00:25:23.370
So I have lots of talent, great
teams, but not enough of them.

00:25:23.370 --> 00:25:26.660
So we really look to, like
others, looking to the cloud.

00:25:26.660 --> 00:25:27.940
How can we leverage cloud?

00:25:27.940 --> 00:25:31.500
We picked Google Cloud, and
I've been pleasantly surprised.

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:33.070
Very happy with
the product so far.

00:25:33.070 --> 00:25:35.215
So a little bit about
the solution, health

00:25:35.215 --> 00:25:36.790
care in the cloud for us.

00:25:36.790 --> 00:25:38.650
Chrome Enterprise is
at the center of that.

00:25:38.650 --> 00:25:41.150
And that's everything from the
Chrome browser on the desktop

00:25:41.150 --> 00:25:46.570
that we standardize to things
like Chromebooks, Chromeboxes,

00:25:46.570 --> 00:25:49.090
the meeting room
boxes as well that we

00:25:49.090 --> 00:25:50.840
use that we're starting to use.

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:54.340
We also use Docs and Sheets,
which are your Word and Excel

00:25:54.340 --> 00:25:55.000
replacements.

00:25:55.000 --> 00:25:56.833
And really, Google did
a nice job with that.

00:25:56.833 --> 00:25:58.375
They're kind of one
of the first ones

00:25:58.375 --> 00:26:00.280
to have that real time
collaboration in Docs

00:26:00.280 --> 00:26:00.780
and Sheets.

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:03.370
And we took advantage of
that early and very powerful.

00:26:03.370 --> 00:26:03.940
Team Drive.

00:26:03.940 --> 00:26:05.315
Another great one
for if you want

00:26:05.315 --> 00:26:07.732
to replace your SharePoint
[INAUDIBLE] SharePoint product.

00:26:07.732 --> 00:26:09.967
We had about 40 different
SharePoint sites out there.

00:26:09.967 --> 00:26:12.550
When we moved to Team Drive, we
were able to consolidate that.

00:26:12.550 --> 00:26:14.920
And we put Team Drive
together with sites.

00:26:14.920 --> 00:26:17.590
Then you can either do a very
nice intranet application

00:26:17.590 --> 00:26:22.000
or have the same functionality
as SharePoint, essentially.

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:22.930
Alert Center.

00:26:22.930 --> 00:26:24.513
You can't do any
kind of presentation,

00:26:24.513 --> 00:26:26.263
especially in health
care, without talking

00:26:26.263 --> 00:26:27.100
about cybersecurity.

00:26:27.100 --> 00:26:28.750
So the nice thing
about Alert Center

00:26:28.750 --> 00:26:31.240
is it's across all the
entire Google platform,

00:26:31.240 --> 00:26:32.590
all the products you're using.

00:26:32.590 --> 00:26:34.215
It's kind of that
alerting to tell you,

00:26:34.215 --> 00:26:35.965
hey, something's going
wrong, whether it's

00:26:35.965 --> 00:26:37.930
on your mobile device,
of suspicious activity

00:26:37.930 --> 00:26:42.160
on a mobile device, to something
going on in the Chrome browser.

00:26:42.160 --> 00:26:43.870
It's kind of all the
things as a consumer

00:26:43.870 --> 00:26:46.180
that you see out there when
you connect from a trusted

00:26:46.180 --> 00:26:46.840
computer.

00:26:46.840 --> 00:26:48.280
Do you trust this
computer or not?

00:26:48.280 --> 00:26:52.270
So that's what this is
for your enterprise.

00:26:52.270 --> 00:26:55.160
So the solutions expanded.

00:26:55.160 --> 00:26:58.210
So I talked a little bit about
the SharePoint sites and silos.

00:26:58.210 --> 00:27:00.160
Team Drive and Google
Sites really working out

00:27:00.160 --> 00:27:01.120
very well for us there.

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:02.745
We were able to
consolidate and get rid

00:27:02.745 --> 00:27:04.450
of our SharePoint footprint.

00:27:04.450 --> 00:27:05.922
Legacy applications.

00:27:05.922 --> 00:27:07.630
So we have to support
these applications.

00:27:07.630 --> 00:27:09.910
And kind of how do we
do that with Chrome?

00:27:09.910 --> 00:27:12.010
And the nice thing,
what I like about Chrome

00:27:12.010 --> 00:27:13.870
is it's a modern
operating system.

00:27:13.870 --> 00:27:15.787
Really a cloud based
operating system.

00:27:15.787 --> 00:27:18.370
But we can still tie it to some
of the traditional ways of how

00:27:18.370 --> 00:27:21.290
we used to do things with VDI or
Virtual Desktop Infrastructure.

00:27:21.290 --> 00:27:23.110
So plug into your provider.

00:27:23.110 --> 00:27:24.430
Google pretty much supports it.

00:27:24.430 --> 00:27:26.140
We are a VMware
Horizon customer,

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:27.515
and it works very
well with that.

00:27:27.515 --> 00:27:29.300
Although it works
with Citrix as well.

00:27:29.300 --> 00:27:30.693
And we found that as very nice.

00:27:30.693 --> 00:27:32.860
So we've been able to kind
of put Chrome devices out

00:27:32.860 --> 00:27:36.010
there and still provide access
to our legacy applications.

00:27:36.010 --> 00:27:37.260
Multiple collaboration tools.

00:27:37.260 --> 00:27:39.100
So things like what I
didn't mention on the slide

00:27:39.100 --> 00:27:40.530
before, Google
Meet and Hangouts.

00:27:40.530 --> 00:27:42.800
So Meet is your
Webex replacement.

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:45.250
It can be used for training,
video conferencing.

00:27:45.250 --> 00:27:45.753
Really nice.

00:27:45.753 --> 00:27:47.170
And the great thing
about it is it

00:27:47.170 --> 00:27:50.470
comes with when you get the
suite, Google Suite, which

00:27:50.470 --> 00:27:51.910
I also have mentioned Gmail.

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:54.310
So our journey started
with Gmail and G Suite.

00:27:54.310 --> 00:27:55.690
But it's all part of suite.

00:27:55.690 --> 00:27:58.030
When you use that, you get
Google Meet and Hangouts.

00:27:58.030 --> 00:28:01.660
So very nice tools that we've
been able take advantage of.

00:28:01.660 --> 00:28:03.750
On the regulatory
side, it doesn't

00:28:03.750 --> 00:28:06.592
get any more important than
the security piece of this.

00:28:06.592 --> 00:28:08.800
What's nice is the security
[INAUDIBLE] in Chrome OS.

00:28:08.800 --> 00:28:12.040
So we can put them out there
without needing expensive tools

00:28:12.040 --> 00:28:12.720
to manage them.

00:28:12.720 --> 00:28:14.500
So they're encrypted
by default. You

00:28:14.500 --> 00:28:16.910
can use the Google Console
to manage all that.

00:28:16.910 --> 00:28:19.603
And I mean, even for things
like software deployment,

00:28:19.603 --> 00:28:22.270
you don't have to go out and get
a separate tool of the software

00:28:22.270 --> 00:28:24.443
deployment to your
chrome devices.

00:28:24.443 --> 00:28:26.110
And we found there's
a reduction in cost

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:30.250
there, because we don't need
those expensive tools anymore.

00:28:30.250 --> 00:28:32.753
So easy patching on
the Chrome browser.

00:28:32.753 --> 00:28:33.670
Angela mentioned that.

00:28:33.670 --> 00:28:34.462
It's auto patching.

00:28:34.462 --> 00:28:35.260
It really is true.

00:28:35.260 --> 00:28:37.372
I was pleasantly
surprised by this.

00:28:37.372 --> 00:28:38.830
Another tool that
we needed to kind

00:28:38.830 --> 00:28:40.270
of patch our
Windows environment.

00:28:40.270 --> 00:28:42.030
Not needed in the
Chrome environment.

00:28:42.030 --> 00:28:46.040
It is self patching,
and it really does work.

00:28:46.040 --> 00:28:47.870
So on the rising costs.

00:28:47.870 --> 00:28:51.045
We estimated about $400 to
$600 per Chromebook deployed.

00:28:51.045 --> 00:28:52.420
And that's because
you don't have

00:28:52.420 --> 00:28:54.253
kind of the expensive
cost of your Microsoft

00:28:54.253 --> 00:28:57.130
licensing for Office
and things like that.

00:28:57.130 --> 00:29:01.600
And along with email, Gmail
did a nice, great cost savings

00:29:01.600 --> 00:29:02.270
for us.

00:29:02.270 --> 00:29:03.280
And they're easy
to use products.

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:04.080
I mean, they really are.

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:05.497
You can really put
them out there.

00:29:05.497 --> 00:29:07.300
When we went live with
Gmail and G Suite,

00:29:07.300 --> 00:29:08.900
we did all the training
to our organization.

00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:10.275
We did all those
kinds of things.

00:29:10.275 --> 00:29:12.730
But to be honest, they had
seen the product already

00:29:12.730 --> 00:29:14.190
in their kind of consumer life.

00:29:14.190 --> 00:29:16.040
So it really helped
with that transition.

00:29:16.040 --> 00:29:18.370
And we found that it's been
an evolutionary process

00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:19.150
since then.

00:29:19.150 --> 00:29:22.360
We just kind put it out there,
and folks just start to use it.

00:29:22.360 --> 00:29:23.795
It's been a great thing.

00:29:23.795 --> 00:29:25.420
So Google Cloud
increased productivity.

00:29:25.420 --> 00:29:26.450
It's easy to use.

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:27.390
It is budget friendly.

00:29:27.390 --> 00:29:29.890
Secure out of the box and does
provide better collaboration.

00:29:29.890 --> 00:29:32.890
All the tools are very tightly
integrated and very powerful

00:29:32.890 --> 00:29:35.450
when you use it as a platform.

00:29:35.450 --> 00:29:37.870
So a little bit of
a case study here.

00:29:37.870 --> 00:29:42.330
In New Jersey back in 2017,
the state required us to do--

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:44.230
they put out a New
Jersey early intervention

00:29:44.230 --> 00:29:45.620
system for child development.

00:29:45.620 --> 00:29:47.110
So the goal of
this was the catch

00:29:47.110 --> 00:29:49.000
developmental
disabilities in children

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:50.740
early in the patient's home.

00:29:50.740 --> 00:29:55.725
And they put this progress
no tracking system out there.

00:29:55.725 --> 00:29:57.350
They required our
clinicians to use it.

00:29:57.350 --> 00:29:58.350
We had to use it
in about 30 days.

00:29:58.350 --> 00:29:59.850
So our director of
child development

00:29:59.850 --> 00:30:01.795
kind of came to me in
a panic saying, hey,

00:30:01.795 --> 00:30:03.170
we need devices
to get out there.

00:30:03.170 --> 00:30:04.550
We need to do this in 30 days.

00:30:04.550 --> 00:30:05.350
How are we going to do it?

00:30:05.350 --> 00:30:07.100
So we were able to do
it with Chromebooks.

00:30:07.100 --> 00:30:08.590
So it was amazing
how fast we were

00:30:08.590 --> 00:30:10.970
able to deploy it since
they're secure out of the box.

00:30:10.970 --> 00:30:12.680
it was a web based application.

00:30:12.680 --> 00:30:14.230
It had everything
that they needed.

00:30:14.230 --> 00:30:15.990
And we were able to
get it out there.

00:30:15.990 --> 00:30:18.695
And then it was very tightly
integrated with our legacy

00:30:18.695 --> 00:30:19.570
applications as well.

00:30:19.570 --> 00:30:22.360
We provided access to Windows
applications through VDI.

00:30:22.360 --> 00:30:24.680
And it had G Suite and
Chromebook or anything else

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:25.680
that they needed to use.

00:30:25.680 --> 00:30:28.510
So kind of a nice story
there and still working out

00:30:28.510 --> 00:30:29.750
very well.

00:30:29.750 --> 00:30:33.600
And always a good thing when
we're helping out children.

00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:37.440
So we talked about
cybersecurity and privacy.

00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:42.780
And this slide really is about
the security piece of this.

00:30:42.780 --> 00:30:45.810
I mean, I think back in 2016,
Verizon put a report out.

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:49.170
90% of the breaches that happen
out there start with email.

00:30:49.170 --> 00:30:54.580
So the G Suite, Gmail
itself very powerful.

00:30:54.580 --> 00:30:55.830
We do a lot.

00:30:55.830 --> 00:30:57.830
I mean, the email
security AI is incredible.

00:30:57.830 --> 00:31:00.040
You get these products kind
of right out of the gate.

00:31:00.040 --> 00:31:02.040
So things like attachment
sandbox and protection

00:31:02.040 --> 00:31:04.310
against 0 day threats.

00:31:04.310 --> 00:31:06.840
So that's basically
a piece of malware

00:31:06.840 --> 00:31:08.470
out there that nobody
knows how to fix.

00:31:08.470 --> 00:31:10.290
And this is technology that's
put out there for Google

00:31:10.290 --> 00:31:12.630
to try to catch it, really,
without any kind of pattern

00:31:12.630 --> 00:31:15.900
or any kind of traditional
signature based technology.

00:31:15.900 --> 00:31:18.420
When we talking about protecting
patient health information,

00:31:18.420 --> 00:31:19.870
encryption at rest is huge.

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:21.698
For us, we knew we
needed to do this.

00:31:21.698 --> 00:31:23.490
We just could not do
it in our data center.

00:31:23.490 --> 00:31:25.860
I mean, the cost for us
to encrypt everything

00:31:25.860 --> 00:31:28.350
that we have in our data center
was really off the charts.

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:29.465
Not really feasible.

00:31:29.465 --> 00:31:30.840
So you get this
by default. And I

00:31:30.840 --> 00:31:32.490
think I heard that Google's
one of the only ones

00:31:32.490 --> 00:31:35.010
that in their cloud environment
they provide encryption

00:31:35.010 --> 00:31:38.480
by default. So that
was very powerful.

00:31:38.480 --> 00:31:40.320
Encrypting data at rest.

00:31:40.320 --> 00:31:42.120
We were able to do,
around regulatory,

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:45.712
the secure, hardened
Chrome OS environment works

00:31:45.712 --> 00:31:46.920
very well for our clinicians.

00:31:46.920 --> 00:31:49.212
And what's nice is the
security, it just kind of works.

00:31:49.212 --> 00:31:52.290
It doesn't get in the way of
the provider or the clinician

00:31:52.290 --> 00:31:56.073
providing care, which is always
a good thing in security.

00:31:56.073 --> 00:31:58.490
The other big factor for us
on the business associate side

00:31:58.490 --> 00:31:59.660
for HIPAA.

00:31:59.660 --> 00:32:02.210
If you're going to do
business with health care,

00:32:02.210 --> 00:32:04.280
you really have to sign
a business associate.

00:32:04.280 --> 00:32:08.440
So Google does sign HIPAA
business associate agreements.

00:32:08.440 --> 00:32:10.633
And that was a key
one for us early

00:32:10.633 --> 00:32:12.050
on when we were
looking at G Suite

00:32:12.050 --> 00:32:13.760
probably about six
or seven years ago.

00:32:13.760 --> 00:32:17.410
They weren't quite there yet,
but now they are doing that.

00:32:17.410 --> 00:32:18.730
So very powerful.

00:32:18.730 --> 00:32:20.240
Strong regulatory compliance.

00:32:20.240 --> 00:32:21.710
Advanced threat protection.

00:32:21.710 --> 00:32:23.630
Cloud based security
that is always improving.

00:32:23.630 --> 00:32:26.042
I mean, you get these things
without thinking about it.

00:32:26.042 --> 00:32:27.500
The features just
kind of come out.

00:32:27.500 --> 00:32:28.760
You do have to turn them on.

00:32:28.760 --> 00:32:31.070
But we truly have seen, at
least on the Google side,

00:32:31.070 --> 00:32:34.850
we've seen the power of cloud
on Google and getting features

00:32:34.850 --> 00:32:36.680
turned on kind of
right away, especially

00:32:36.680 --> 00:32:38.630
in the security part of it.

00:32:38.630 --> 00:32:41.380
[MUSIC PLAYING]

